  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid conditions return this weekend \nwith at least periodic chances for some showers and scattered \nthunderstorms Saturday afternoon through Sunday.\n\nA weak secondary cold front will push south through the western \nSouthern Tier with just the chance for a few isolated showers there \nthis afternoon. Otherwise, high pressure will build over the region \nwith dry, cooler, and less humid conditions through the end of the \nwork week. Considerable clearing, weak or calm surface winds,\nand lingering low-level moisture from recent rainfall should\nyield patchy radiation fog inland from Lakes Erie and Ontario,\nand patchy steam fog in the lake and river valleys of the\nWestern Southern Tier, Finger Lakes, and Eastern Lake Ontario\nRegion around daybreak Friday.\n\nSurface high pressure will move east into New England, with a \nsoutherly return flow advecting a warmer and more humid airmass back \nin across western and north-central NY from southwest to \nnortheast through the day Saturday. This may lead to some \nscattered diurnally driven convection across western NY Saturday\nafternoon, particularly across the interior Southern Tier where\nthe best moisture will be, along with somewhat better forcing \ncloser to a mid level shortwave passing by just to our \nsoutheast. A cold front will then cross the area sometime on \nSunday...timing is still in question. This will bring the next \nbest chance for a round of showers and scattered storms. At this\npoint wind shear and instability both appear to be modest, thus\nthe severe threat looks low at this time. However, if the \nfrontal timing comes across the area at/near peak heating, the \nthunderstorm potential could be greater. Will continue to \nmonitor.",
